A||||7||PAUL88GT||Removing spider webs front facia||||5||
Z||000000||PAUL88GT||07-10-2001||12:46 PM||||I am prepping my 86se for painting.  What is the best way to remove the spider web paint cracking on the front facia?  I have a DA sander.  What grit paper should I use?  Should I sand all the cracked paint off or will the new paint cover over the cracks?  If I remove all the paint, how should I prep the bare surface for painting? Sorry for all the questions.  I am a rookie at body work.  TIA.<BR>Paul||||reg||5
Z||000001||WisconsinGT||07-10-2001||03:11 PM||||I have been told that the best solution for this is to get a new front facia. I saw a Fiero that had a new paint job. (They sanded all the paint off.) After three months, the webbing came back.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||07-10-2001||03:53 PM||||WisconsinGT, chances are that guy didn't get what he paid for.<P>You have to sand all of the color off and get down to the original primer/sealer or past the cracks there should be no reason for the cracks to come back.<P>Depending on how fast you want to remove the paint use anywhere between 320-500.  Use a grit you are comfortable with to start, if it doesn't seem to be cutting fast enough try a coarser grit and vice-a-versa.  <P>When you have the bumper painted, have it shot with primer/sealer before the base/clear and you shouldn't have any problems.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rob Durham||07-10-2001||04:23 PM||||You need to take the paint down to the bumper then use a flexable primer, Dupont makes a good filler primer(URO 1120S)that you can add the flex agent(Plas-Stick 2350S) to. <P>You will also have to add a flex agent to the Base coat and the clear, check to see what flex agent the manufacture recomends||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||07-10-2001||04:57 PM||||never mind Im not saying a word !!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||07-10-2001||10:59 PM||||C'mon Roger, if anyone has the answer for this, it'd be you. I know your opinion on flex agents, but what about the cracks? Is sanding and sealing the trick? I'm geting ready to get my car painted and I want good results. Holler back. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Galen||07-11-2001||07:03 AM||||Whats this about a flex agent? I got no cracks in mine, cept a few. Is this guy coming and flexing the front of my car? stupid goverment. - I just sanded mine down and painted..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/redcreature.gif">   <BR><FONT SIZE="1">To whom it may concern, The preceeding message was the opinion of this person and not of this forum. If you do not like it. Oh well</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||07-11-2001||12:09 PM||||OK. Ive been painting since 1960's. Have my own shop for ........forever. I NEVER EVER use flex agent. It wont do anything but pad the paint manufacturers pocket. If the plastic is cracked itself, nothing you do will cover it up except an all over coat of bumper repair filler. Usually though its just in the paint. make sure you sand all the cracked area to bare plastic. I just use primer-surfacer myself, but epoxy primer may be better. then I just paint it with same paint when I paint the car whether its laquer, enamel, basecoat/clearcoat. Ive very seldom had any problem unless they run into something afterwards. but then its gonna need redone anyway. Lots of guys here swear by flex, but I think its more in their heads. Dont believe every guy in the paint store either, there job is to sell you more stuff.||||reg||1
